.TH XPROXY\-REPLAY 8 "" "Xproxy" "System administration"
.nh
.ad l
.SH "NAME"
.PP
xproxy\-replay \- read and show a recorded session
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBxproxy\-replay\fR [\fB\-summary\fR] [\fB\-html\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-png\fR \fIDIR\fR] [\fB\-at\fR \fIDURATION\fR] [\fB\-every\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-max\-frames\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-speed\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-input\fR] [\fB\-plain\fR] \fIFILE\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBxproxy\-replay\fR reads a session recording written by \fBxgate\fR(8) \[u2014] the SSH, telnet, VNC and RDP gateways \[u2014] or by \fBxrelay\fR(8)\(aqs FTP relay, and shows it.
.PP
A recording of a session made of text is replayed to the terminal, with the escape sequences that reach outside the replay filtered out: a recording carries whatever the far end sent, and handing that straight to a reviewer\(aqs terminal is the one way reading a recording can hurt.
.PP
A recording of a graphical session is a protocol stream rather than a video, which is the deliberate trade the gateways make: decoding at capture time would mean implementing every encoding a desktop might choose and silently losing the rest. This program is the decoder written against the file afterwards. For RFB (\fB*.rfb.cast\fR) it rebuilds the framebuffer and can write PNG frames or one self\-contained page that plays them. For RDP (\fB*.rdp.cast\fR) it reads the framing, the channels and the marks, and says plainly that the graphics are not decoded rather than drawing something nobody sent.
.PP
It opens no sockets, runs nothing, and writes only where it is told.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(0.9i) lbx
lw(0.9i) lx.
T{
Option
T}	T{
Description
T}
T{
\fB\-\:summary\fR
T}	T{
Print what the stream did \[u2014] every rectangle with its encoding and size,\: the totals,\: and every mark \[u2014] instead of replaying it.
T}
T{
\fB\-\:html\fR \fIFILE\fR
T}	T{
Write one self\-contained page that plays the session: the frames,\: the recorded timing,\: and the marks beside them. No network,\: no scripts from anywhere else.
T}
T{
\fB\-\:png\fR \fIDIR\fR
T}	T{
Write one PNG per framebuffer update into a directory that must already exist.
T}
T{
\fB\-\:at\fR \fIDURATION\fR
T}	T{
With \fB\-\:png\fR,\: write only the screen as it was at that point of the session.
T}
T{
\fB\-\:every\fR \fIN\fR
T}	T{
Keep one frame in this many updates.
T}
T{
\fB\-\:max\-\:frames\fR \fIN\fR
T}	T{
Stop after this many frames. Default 2000: a session of ten thousand updates is not a page anything opens.
T}
T{
\fB\-\:speed\fR \fIN\fR
T}	T{
Multiply the recorded timing when replaying a session of text.
T}
T{
\fB\-\:input\fR
T}	T{
Include what the client sent,\: where the recording holds it (\fBrecording.\:input\fR).
T}
T{
\fB\-\:plain\fR
T}	T{
Drop every escape sequence rather than keeping the ones that draw.
T}
T{
\fB\-\:version\fR
T}	T{
Print the version and exit.
T}
.TE
.SH "ENCODINGS"
.PP
Decoded: Raw, CopyRect, RRE, CoRRE, Hextile, TRLE and ZRLE, which is what RFC 6143 specifies, with the desktop\-size and last\-rect pseudo\-encodings and the two cursor ones read to keep the stream in step.
.PP
Not decoded: Tight and the vendors\(aq own. They are not in RFC 6143, Tight carries JPEG and its own compression streams, and a player that guessed would be inventing a picture inside an investigation. Such a rectangle stops the decoding, is counted, and is named in the output.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.PP
0 on success, 1 on an error, 2 on a usage mistake.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.PP
Replay a bastion session as it happened:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
xproxy\-replay /var/log/xgate/rec/session\-20260101\-120000\-alice.cast
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
What a VNC session\(aqs stream did, without drawing it:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
xproxy\-replay \-summary session\-20260101\-120000\-alice.rfb.cast
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
A page a reviewer can open with no network:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
xproxy\-replay \-html /tmp/session.html session\-20260101\-120000\-alice.rfb.cast
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
The screen as it was twelve seconds in:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
xproxy\-replay \-at 12s \-png /tmp/frames session\-20260101\-120000\-alice.rfb.cast
.ft
.fi
.RE
